Stage C (C1, blocker B5): the GameSpy SDK's Linux thread shim calls
pthread_cancel, which bionic deliberately does not provide (Android's
clang defines __linux__, so the SDK selects its Linux shim and the
engine build fails with "call to undeclared function 'pthread_cancel'"
at gsthreadlinux.c:72). On Android, detach instead: GameSpy's worker
threads run on socket timeouts and exit on their own, so the handle is
abandoned rather than force-killed. Semantic delta recorded in
stage-c-c1-plan.md (B5).

This is the formalisation B5 asked for: the probe workflow applies
this file to the vendored GameSpy tree right after Tools/vendor.sh
installs it (same git-apply-into-the-destination mechanics upstream's
own vendor.sh uses for gamespy-gsi-unix.patch), so a vendor re-fetch
keeps the change. Paths are relative to the GameSpy destination root
(Libraries/Source/GameSpy).

diff --git a/src/common/linux/gsthreadlinux.c b/src/common/linux/gsthreadlinux.c
--- a/src/common/linux/gsthreadlinux.c
+++ b/src/common/linux/gsthreadlinux.c
@@ -66,13 +66,22 @@
 void gsiCancelThread(GSIThreadID id)
 {
     //should i destroy the attributes here?
     pthread_attr_destroy(&id.attr);
 
+#if defined(__ANDROID__)
+    // Stage C (C1): bionic has no pthread_cancel, by design. Detach instead:
+    // GameSpy's worker threads run on socket timeouts and exit on their own,
+    // so the handle is abandoned rather than force-killed. Semantic delta
+    // recorded in stage-c-c1-plan.md (B5); to be formalised as a vendor patch
+    // beside gamespy-gsi-unix.patch so a vendor.sh re-fetch keeps it.
+    pthread_detach(id.thread);
+#else
     if (pthread_cancel(id.thread) != PTHREAD_NO_ERROR) {
         //there was an error - how should we handle these? or should we?
         gsDebugFormat(GSIDebugCat_Common, GSIDebugType_Misc, GSIDebugLevel_WarmError, "Failed to cancel thread\r\n");
     }
+#endif
     //free up memory and set to NULL
 
     gsifree(&id.thread);
 }
